    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">
      How it Works
    </h2>

    <p>
      The Differential Equation Visualizer combines symbolic mathematics with
      numerical methods to turn user-entered equations into graphical
      solutions. The original application was built around a keypad-based
      Tkinter interface that allowed equations to be constructed without
      requiring users to type mathematical syntax directly.
    </p>

    <p>
      When an equation is entered, the application first converts the
      displayed expression into a form that can be interpreted
      computationally. SymPy's <code>parse_expr()</code> function and symbolic
      variables are then used to identify the structure of the equation. The
      program distinguishes between ordinary expressions, explicit equations,
      first-order differential equations, and second-order differential
      equations.
    </p>

    <p>
      For first- and second-order differential equations, SymPy is used to
      algebraically rearrange the equation and solve for the highest
      derivative. The resulting symbolic expression is converted into a
      numerical function using <code>lambdify()</code>. This allows the
      symbolic result to be evaluated efficiently with NumPy during numerical
      integration.
    </p>

    <p>
      The numerical solution is then generated using SciPy's
      <code>odeint()</code> solver. For a first-order equation, the program
      integrates the equation using the specified initial value. For a
      second-order equation, the equation is rewritten as a system of two
      first-order equations, using both the function value and its first
      derivative as the initial conditions. The solution is evaluated over
      1,001 evenly spaced points across the selected x-range.
    </p>

    <p>
      The application also supports several special functions. A unit-step
      function is implemented using NumPy's conditional array operations,
      while the Dirac delta function is approximated with a narrow Gaussian.
      These functions are passed into the numerical functions generated by
      SymPy, allowing them to be incorporated directly into differential
      equations.
    </p>

    <p>
      For first-order differential equations, the application additionally
      generates a slope field. A grid of points is created across the selected
      x and y ranges, and the differential equation is evaluated at each point
      to determine the local slope. These slopes are normalized and plotted as
      directional vectors, providing a visual representation of the equation's
      behavior alongside its numerical solution.
    </p>

    <p>
      Finally, Matplotlib is used to render the solution graph. The graph
      includes coordinate axes and a grid, and first-order solutions can be
      overlaid on their corresponding slope field. This creates a direct
      visual connection between the differential equation, its local behavior,
      and the resulting numerical solution.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">
      Code Highlights
    </h2>

    <p>
      The project combines several different computational techniques. SymPy
      handles symbolic parsing and algebraic manipulation, SciPy performs
      numerical integration, NumPy provides numerical array operations, and
      Matplotlib handles visualization. The primary function for handling equation solving and plotting is shown below.
    </p>

    <Code
      code={`def graphInput(funcInput1, funcInput2, funcInput3, funcInput4, fig, canvas,
               x):  # Function that graphs an inputted equation with error handling
    global IVP1, IVP2
    input_value = funcInput3.get("1.0", tk.END).strip()
    if input_value:
        XL = float(input_value)
    else:
        XL = 0
    input_value_2 = funcInput4.get("1.0", tk.END).strip()
    if input_value_2:
        XR = float(input_value_2)
    else:
        XR = 1
    inputString = funcInput2.get("1.0", tk.END)
    try:
        if "y2" in inputString:
            title = str(funcInput1.get("1.0", tk.END))
            plt.title(title)
            inputString = inputString.replace("np.", "")
            lhs = parse_expr(inputString.split("=", 1)[0])
            rhs = parse_expr(inputString.split("=", 1)[1])
            # Define symbolic variables
            x, y2, y1, y0 = symbols('x y2 y1 y0')

            # Define the differential equation
            differential_equation = Eq(lhs, rhs)

            # Solve for y'' (y2)
            y2 = solve(differential_equation, y2)[0]  # Extract y''

            # Convert the symbolic solution into a numerical function
            y2_function = lambdify((x, y0, y1), y2, modules=['numpy', {'step_function': step_function},
                                                             {'impulse_function': impulse_function}])

            # Initial conditions: [y(0), y'(0)]
            init_conditions = [IVP1, IVP2]

            # Time points
            time = np.linspace(XL, XR, 1001)

            # Define the system of ODEs using the solved equation
            def system_of_odes(y, t):
                y0, y1 = y  # y1 = y, y2 = y'
                x = t
                y2 = y2_function(x, y0, y1)
                return y1, y2

            # Solve the ODE numerically
            solution = odeint(system_of_odes, init_conditions, time)
            drawChart(fig, canvas, time, solution[:, 0], XL, XR, None)
            title = funcInput1.get("1.0", tk.END).replace("*", "")
            plt.title(title + "y(" + str(XL) + ") = " + str(IVP1) + ", y'(" + str(XL) + ") = " + str(IVP2))
            deleteText(funcInput1)
            deleteText(funcInput2)
        elif "y1" in inputString:
            inputString = inputString.replace("np.", "")
            lhs = parse_expr(inputString.split("=", 1)[0])
            rhs = parse_expr(inputString.split("=", 1)[1])
            x, y1, y0 = symbols('x, y1 y0')
            differential_equation = Eq(lhs, rhs)
            y1 = solve(differential_equation, y1)[0]
            y1_function = lambdify((x, y0), y1, modules=['numpy', {'step_function': step_function},
                                                         {'impulse_function': impulse_function}])
            init_condition = IVP1
            time = np.linspace(XL, XR, 1001)

            def system_of_odes(y, t):
                y0 = y
                x = t
                y1 = y1_function(x, y0)
                return y1

            solution = odeint(system_of_odes, init_condition, time)
            drawChart(fig, canvas, time, solution[:], XL, XR, y1_function)
            title = funcInput1.get("1.0", tk.END).replace("*", "")
            plt.title(title + "y(" + str(XL) + ") = " + str(IVP1))
            deleteText(funcInput1)
            deleteText(funcInput2)
        elif "y0" in inputString:
            inputString = inputString.replace("np.", "")
            lhs = parse_expr(inputString.split("=", 1)[0])
            rhs = parse_expr(inputString.split("=", 1)[1])
            x, y0 = symbols('x, y0')
            equation = Eq(lhs, rhs)
            y0 = solve(equation, y0)
            print(type(y0))
            print(y0)
            y1_function = lambdify(x, y0[0], modules=['numpy', {'step_function': step_function},
                                                         {'impulse_function': impulse_function}])
            x = np.linspace(XL, XR, 1001)
            y = y1_function(x)#np.full_like(x, y0)
            drawChart(fig, canvas, x, y, XL, XR, None)
            title = funcInput1.get("1.0", tk.END).replace("*", "")
            plt.title(title)
            deleteText(funcInput1)
            deleteText(funcInput2)
        else:
            x = np.linspace(XL, XR, 1001)
            y = np.full_like(x, eval(inputString))
            drawChart(fig, canvas, x, y, XL, XR, None)
            title = funcInput1.get("1.0", tk.END).replace("*", "")
            plt.title(title)
            deleteText(funcInput1)
            deleteText(funcInput2)

    except SyntaxError:
        errorMessage()
        deleteText(funcInput1)
        deleteText(funcInput2)
    `}
      lang="python"
      theme="github-dark"
      wrap
