    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-2">
      Engineering Challenges
    </h2>

    <p>
      One of the primary challenges was parsing user-entered mathematical equations into a form that could be processed computationally. The calculator had to support both familiar mathematical notation and the syntax required by the underlying Python libraries. I used SymPy's expression parser to interpret equations symbolically, then separated the equation into its left- and right-hand sides so that differential equations could be rearranged and solved for the required derivative. This allowed the same interface to handle algebraic equations as well as first- and second-order differential equations.
    </p>

    <p>
      Another challenge was generating useful visualizations from the parsed equations. In addition to plotting the resulting solution, the application could generate slope fields for first-order differential equations. The slope field was created by evaluating the differential equation over a grid of (x) and (y) values, normalizing the resulting direction vectors, and displaying them using Matplotlib's vector plotting functionality. The calculated solution could then be plotted alongside the slope field, providing a visual way to compare the numerical solution with the behavior predicted by the differential equation.
    </p>

    <p>
      A final challenge was solving differential equations numerically while supporting different orders of equations. For first-order equations, the parsed expression was converted into a numerical function using SymPy's lambdify and then solved with SciPy's odeint. Second-order equations required an additional step: the equation was first solved symbolically for the second derivative and then rewritten as a system of two first-order equations. The initial conditions were supplied by the user, and the resulting system was passed to the numerical solver to generate the solution across the selected range. This approach allowed the calculator to handle a variety of differential equations while keeping the user-facing interface relatively simple.
    </p>

